<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as John Morley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn, was a British Liberal politician, journalist and writer. A close ally and biographer of William Gladstone, he served as Chief Secretary for Ireland and, from 1905 to 1910, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, when with the Viceroy Lord Minto he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-councils-act-1909">Morley–Minto reforms</a> of 1909. He resigned from the cabinet in 1914 in protest against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s entry into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-morley-1st-viscount-morley-of-blackburn.jpg" alt="John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W. &amp; D. Downey · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morley-John-Viscount.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Morley was born on 24 December 1838 in Blackburn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, the son of the surgeon Jonathan Morley. He was educated at Queen Elizabeth's Grammar School in Blackburn and Cheltenham College. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> he quarrelled with his father, who wanted him to become a clergyman, over religion, and left without an honours degree; he alluded to the rift in On Compromise (1874). Called to the bar by Lincoln's Inn in 1873, he turned to journalism and later called leaving the law his long enduring regret.</p><p>He edited the Pall Mall Gazette from 1880 to 1883, with W. T. Stead as his assistant. After failing at Blackburn in 1869 and Westminster in 1880, he was elected Liberal MP for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle upon Tyne</a> at a by-election in February 1883.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A Gladstonian Liberal, Morley became Chief Secretary for Ireland in February 1886 and lost the post in July when Gladstone's government fell over Home Rule. He held it again from 1892, a tenure that was hardly a success, as the Irish gentry made things difficult for an avowed Home Ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin-castle">Dublin Castle</a>. He lost his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne">Newcastle</a> seat in 1895 and won Montrose Burghs at a by-election in February 1896.</p><p>From 1889 he resisted labour leaders' demands for a legal eight-hour day, which he said would be like thrusting an Act of Parliament like a ramrod into the machinery of British industry, and it brought him the hostility of John Burns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keir-hardie">Keir Hardie</a> and Robert Blatchford.</p><p>In December 1905 Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman made him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, though he would have preferred the Exchequer. In May 1907 he sanctioned extreme measures against sedition, but he also appointed two Indians to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-india">Council of India</a> and took steps towards decentralisation. He became Viscount Morley of Blackburn in 1908, the first peer to decline a coat of arms. As a peer he helped secure the Parliament Act 1911, and he was Lord President of the Council until 1914. On 2 August 1914 he resigned with John Burns, hostile to fighting alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Morley's monographs on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> (1872), Rousseau (1873), Diderot and the Encyclopaedists (1878), Burke (1879) and Walpole (1889) made his name as a writer, and he edited Macmillan's English Men of Letters series. His Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a> (1903) sold more than 25,000 copies in its first year. He published Recollections in 1917. He died on 23 September 1923 at Flowermead, Wimbledon Park, and the viscountcy became extinct. He married Rose Mary Ayling in 1870 and they had no children. Mahomed Ali Jinnah was among those he inspired.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Morley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Blackburn, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.75,-2.48&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Blackburn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4495 318 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Morley, 1st Viscount Morley of Blackburn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4945 588h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4945 588h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.75,-2.48&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
